1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,102
Three questions a CTO can ask
after an ordinary release week.

2
00:00:08,442 --> 00:00:13,820
One. "Both pull requests were green. main
is red. The two branches did not touch a

3
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:15,670
single file in common. How?"

4
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:20,201
Two. "Ravi's retry setting disappeared
from the config in a merge commit.

5
00:00:20,241 --> 00:00:25,013
git log -p shows no diff for that commit.
Who removed the line, and how do we prove

6
00:00:25,053 --> 00:00:25,244
it?"

7
00:00:25,584 --> 00:00:29,496
Three. "We reverted the bad merge on
Friday. On Monday we merged the repaired

8
00:00:29,536 --> 00:00:32,780
branch, the merge was clean, and
half of the feature is missing.

9
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:33,652
Where did it go?"

10
00:00:33,992 --> 00:00:37,517
None of the three needs a bug in Git.
Each follows from what a merge is.

11
00:00:37,557 --> 00:00:41,181
This video answers the first two and
shows you the mechanism of the third.

12
00:00:41,221 --> 00:00:43,211
Keep count. We'll tick them off as we go.

13
00:00:45,051 --> 00:00:47,986
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:48,026 --> 00:00:51,886
A merge joins another branch's history
into yours. This is the last video of the

15
00:00:51,926 --> 00:00:54,714
merge module, and it's about
the merges that did not stop.

16
00:00:54,754 --> 00:00:58,713
A conflict is Git asking for help. A clean
merge is Git not asking, and that's the

17
00:00:58,753 --> 00:01:00,760
more dangerous case, because nobody looks.

18
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:06,918
Three parts. First, why "no conflicts" is
not "correct", with a merge of two green

19
00:01:06,958 --> 00:01:11,132
branches, branches whose checks
pass, that fails its check.

20
00:01:11,172 --> 00:01:14,775
Second, how to audit a merge
commit after the fact.

21
00:01:14,815 --> 00:01:18,561
A merge commit is a commit
with more than one parent.

22
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:23,740
It stores a tree, one complete snapshot
of the files, and no diff, so the default

23
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:28,439
log shows you nothing. Third, git
merge-tree, which computes a merge without a

24
00:01:28,479 --> 00:01:33,318
working tree, that is, without files on
disk. That's how you dry-run a merge, and

25
00:01:33,358 --> 00:01:36,450
it's how stock Git exposes
the strategy to a server.

26
00:01:36,790 --> 00:01:42,220
At the end there are three pointers to
later chapters: rerere, reverting a merge,

27
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:44,448
and an option that Git 2.56 adds.

28
00:01:46,288 --> 00:01:47,867
Here's what you'll walk away with.

29
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:49,619
After this video you can:

30
00:01:49,959 --> 00:01:54,582
Explain why two green branches can merge
without conflict into a broken result.

31
00:01:54,922 --> 00:01:58,539
Show what a merge resolution
changed with --remerge-diff.

32
00:01:58,879 --> 00:02:00,747
Say what git show displays for a
merge commit and what it hides.

33
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:04,052
Compute a merge without a working
tree with git merge-tree.

34
00:02:04,392 --> 00:02:10,773
Name the follow-up topics: rerere, reverting
a merge, and git add --resolved in Git

35
00:02:10,813 --> 00:02:11,160
2.56.

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,755
Start with the one sentence from section
8.15. A merge without conflicts means that

37
00:02:19,795 --> 00:02:26,304
no two text changes overlapped. It says
nothing about whether the result builds,

38
00:02:26,344 --> 00:02:28,924
passes its tests or makes sense.

39
00:02:29,264 --> 00:02:34,335
Recall the idea from the start of the
chapter. A merge is computed from exactly

40
00:02:34,375 --> 00:02:37,959
three snapshots, and Git
compares lines of text in them.

41
00:02:37,999 --> 00:02:43,394
It doesn't know what the lines mean. So
there is a class of conflict that Git cannot

42
00:02:43,434 --> 00:02:48,829
see by construction: one change depends on
another, and the dependency lives outside

43
00:02:48,869 --> 00:02:53,358
the text of any one file. The textbook
calls these semantic conflicts.

44
00:02:53,698 --> 00:03:00,578
Ravi renames a function. Asha, from the same
starting commit, adds a new module that

45
00:03:00,618 --> 00:03:06,921
calls the old name. Different files, no
overlap, clean merge, broken program.

46
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:13,758
Today's demo is that story: d1c3cff is the
rename, 5008877 is the new caller, and M

47
00:03:13,798 --> 00:03:14,994
is their merge.

48
00:03:15,334 --> 00:03:19,024
There is a quiet variant, and
it is worse than the loud one.

49
00:03:19,064 --> 00:03:24,122
If one branch renames a configuration key
and another adds code that reads the old

50
00:03:24,162 --> 00:03:29,158
key with a default, nothing fails. An
evaluation run uses the default, the scores

51
00:03:29,198 --> 00:03:33,573
shift, and the merge that caused it
shows no conflict and no red check.

52
00:03:33,913 --> 00:03:38,842
A second way a clean merge surprises people:
a change that was made on both sides

53
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:43,627
and reverted on one. You will see it
return without a word, and the rule table

54
00:03:43,667 --> 00:03:44,363
explains it.

55
00:03:44,703 --> 00:03:49,824
Then the term "evil merge". The glossary
definition is: an evil merge is a merge

56
00:03:49,864 --> 00:03:53,501
that introduces changes that
do not appear in any parent.

57
00:03:53,541 --> 00:03:58,727
It happens through a resolution that also
changes something outside the conflict,

58
00:03:58,767 --> 00:04:04,081
through an edit between git merge --no-commit
and the commit, or through git commit

59
00:04:04,121 --> 00:04:05,307
--amend on a merge.

60
00:04:05,647 --> 00:04:09,686
The name is harsher than the practice.
Fixing a semantic conflict inside the merge

61
00:04:09,726 --> 00:04:12,969
commit is such a change, and it
keeps every mainline commit green.

62
00:04:13,009 --> 00:04:16,600
git bisect, the search for the commit
that changed something, needs that.

63
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:17,744
The cost is visibility.

64
00:04:18,084 --> 00:04:21,197
Which brings you to auditing. A merge commit
stores a tree and no diff, so "what did

65
00:04:21,237 --> 00:04:24,199
this merge do" has to be computed, and
Git offers three computations that answer

66
00:04:24,239 --> 00:04:25,175
three different questions.

67
00:04:25,515 --> 00:04:29,785
The remerge diff is the one to remember.
Git repeats the merge into a temporary

68
00:04:29,825 --> 00:04:34,041
tree, conflict markers included, and
diffs that tree against the recorded one.

69
00:04:34,081 --> 00:04:36,659
Read it as "what a human
did after Git stopped".

70
00:04:36,999 --> 00:04:42,367
Finally git merge-tree --write-tree. It
performs a real merge with the same strategy

71
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:47,711
as git merge, using only the object database,
Git's store of all objects: no index,

72
00:04:47,751 --> 00:04:52,475
no working tree and no ref update. It
prints the ID of the resulting tree.

73
00:04:52,815 --> 00:04:56,636
Its exit status is 0 for a clean
merge and 1 for a conflict.

74
00:04:56,676 --> 00:05:02,041
It creates objects and changes nothing else,
so it works in a bare repository, where

75
00:05:02,081 --> 00:05:07,447
git merge cannot run. A bare repository is
one with no working tree, like the one on

76
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:08,026
a server.

77
00:05:09,866 --> 00:05:14,767
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy: a
spell-checker passes two paragraphs that

78
00:05:14,807 --> 00:05:18,517
contradict each other, because
every word is spelled correctly.

79
00:05:18,557 --> 00:05:21,970
A clean merge is a document
that passed the spell-checker.

80
00:05:22,310 --> 00:05:27,128
Where it breaks: only in degree. Git checks
even less than a spell-checker does.

81
00:05:27,168 --> 00:05:30,347
It checks one property, the
overlap of changed lines.

82
00:05:30,387 --> 00:05:34,659
It has no parser, no type checker and
no test runner, for any language.

83
00:05:34,999 --> 00:05:39,954
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
two branch tips and the merge commit that

84
00:05:39,994 --> 00:05:43,343
joins them. Tick every commit
whose tree somebody tested.

85
00:05:43,383 --> 00:05:45,662
Which one has no tick? Say it out loud.

86
00:05:50,502 --> 00:05:54,990
The merge has no tick. So the model for the
rest of your career: a merge commit is a

87
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:58,009
new tree that nobody has
tested. Each parent was tested.

88
00:05:58,049 --> 00:06:00,866
The merge result was not,
unless you arranged for it.

89
00:06:00,906 --> 00:06:05,178
And a merge commit is a change in its own
right, which deserves a review, with a

90
00:06:05,218 --> 00:06:06,364
tool that can show it.

91
00:06:08,204 --> 00:06:12,869
Here's your drawing with today's commits.
Each tip passes its check, and M fails.

92
00:06:13,209 --> 00:06:17,209
And the root-cause box for the first
question. It comes back in the demo.

93
00:06:19,049 --> 00:06:23,075
Part 1: clean and wrong. Two
branches from the same commit.

94
00:06:23,115 --> 00:06:24,936
git merge 🟡 CAUTION, twice.

95
00:06:25,276 --> 00:06:29,296
Both merge without a conflict.
Predict the result of the check.

96
00:06:29,336 --> 00:06:31,036
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

97
00:06:35,876 --> 00:06:40,713
It fails: the leaderboard imports accuracy,
which metrics.py no longer defines.

98
00:06:40,753 --> 00:06:44,047
Now the same check on each
parent of the merge commit.

99
00:06:44,387 --> 00:06:49,908
Both parents pass. The merge fails. List
what each side changed since the merge

100
00:06:49,948 --> 00:06:50,260
base.

101
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,881
No path in common. By the rule table, a path
changed on one side only is taken as it

102
00:06:55,921 --> 00:07:00,061
is. No file-level merge ran. There
was nothing for Git to compare.

103
00:07:00,401 --> 00:07:05,004
That's question one, answered. The repair
is a normal, reviewable commit on top of

104
00:07:05,044 --> 00:07:05,570
the merge.

105
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:14,598
Part 2: changed on both sides, reverted on
one. labs/run ch08/revert-one-side, as a

106
00:07:14,638 --> 00:07:21,854
pointer. A fix, max_tokens 2048, was
applied to main and to a branch.

107
00:07:21,894 --> 00:07:26,166
main then reverted it, for a good reason.

108
00:07:26,506 --> 00:07:30,762
main says 512. Predict the
value after merging the branch.

109
00:07:35,602 --> 00:07:47,408
The value is 2048. The reverted change is
back, without a conflict and without a

110
00:07:47,448 --> 00:07:48,592
message.

111
00:07:54,132 --> 00:07:58,873
Base 512, ours 512, theirs 2048:
the row "only theirs changed it".

112
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:04,378
The apply-then-revert pair on main
cancels out, and the merge never sees it.

113
00:08:04,418 --> 00:08:10,029
On screen, the merge base is 6ae3c51 and
the two tips are a2a0277 and 4fac9ee.

114
00:08:10,069 --> 00:08:16,041
Nothing in the rule looks at 523ed40. The
manual describes this behavior and admits

115
00:08:16,081 --> 00:08:22,054
that some people find it confusing. If the
revert must win, say so in the tree that

116
00:08:22,094 --> 00:08:27,197
the merge will see: revert the change
on the branch too before merging.

117
00:08:27,537 --> 00:08:33,332
Part 3: auditing. labs/run ch08/audit-merge.
The history has three merges.

118
00:08:33,372 --> 00:08:39,877
One conflict resolved by keeping our
temperature. One clean merge in which somebody

119
00:08:39,917 --> 00:08:44,450
also set retries to 0, a line
that neither branch touched.

120
00:08:44,490 --> 00:08:50,443
And one honest clean merge. All commands
in this part are 🟢 SAFE: they read.

121
00:08:50,783 --> 00:08:53,814
Predict what git log -p
prints for a merge commit.

122
00:08:58,654 --> 00:09:02,181
The subject line and nothing
else, with -p and with --stat.

123
00:09:02,221 --> 00:09:05,326
By default git log shows no
diff for a merge commit.

124
00:09:05,366 --> 00:09:08,410
That is the "no diff" of
the CTO's second question.

125
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:13,901
git show uses the dense
combined format for merges.

126
00:09:13,941 --> 00:09:20,518
Quick quiz. 70bae5f is the merge
with the hand-resolved conflict.

127
00:09:20,558 --> 00:09:25,709
Will git show print that
resolution? A, yes. B, no.

128
00:09:25,749 --> 00:09:26,931
Your answer?

129
00:09:31,771 --> 00:09:36,005
It's B. For 70bae5f, the merge with the
hand-resolved conflict, git show prints

130
00:09:36,045 --> 00:09:38,764
nothing. If you said yes,
that's the natural guess.

131
00:09:38,804 --> 00:09:43,146
The dense combined format omits hunks where
the result equals one of the parents,

132
00:09:43,186 --> 00:09:47,583
and that resolution took our side exactly.
For fc83756 it shows the smuggled line,

133
00:09:47,623 --> 00:09:51,749
with two minus signs and two plus signs,
because the result differs from both

134
00:09:51,789 --> 00:09:52,181
parents.

135
00:09:52,521 --> 00:09:53,701
Now the remerge diff.

136
00:09:54,041 --> 00:09:59,032
Three merges, three readings. 9a6385a: no
output, so the merge is exactly what Git

137
00:09:59,072 --> 00:10:03,634
computes on its own. 70bae5f: removed
marker lines with one candidate kept.

138
00:10:03,674 --> 00:10:06,824
That's a resolution, and
you can see which side won.

139
00:10:06,864 --> 00:10:11,119
fc83756: a change with no markers
nearby. It came from neither branch.

140
00:10:11,159 --> 00:10:14,187
This is the view that
answers the second question.

141
00:10:14,527 --> 00:10:18,393
The first-parent view answers the other
question, "what did main receive".

142
00:10:21,733 --> 00:10:25,702
The README from the branch and the
retries line, as one ordinary diff.

143
00:10:25,742 --> 00:10:30,168
This view cannot tell you which part is
branch content and which is hand-made.

144
00:10:30,208 --> 00:10:31,370
The remerge diff can.

145
00:10:31,710 --> 00:10:36,776
Two limits from the textbook. The remerge
diff repeats the merge with default

146
00:10:36,816 --> 00:10:42,280
settings, so a merge made with -X ours or
-s ours shows up as a diff, however clean

147
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:47,453
it looked when it was made. And it needs
Git 2.36 or later, handles two-parent

148
00:10:47,493 --> 00:10:52,824
merges only, and its output format is
declared "subject to change" by the manual.

149
00:10:52,864 --> 00:10:54,415
Read it. Don't parse it.

150
00:10:54,755 --> 00:10:59,407
Part 4: git merge-tree. labs/run
ch08/merge-tree. A bare clone stands in for a

151
00:10:59,447 --> 00:10:59,828
server.

152
00:11:00,168 --> 00:11:03,662
git merge refuses: this operation
must be run in a work tree.

153
00:11:03,702 --> 00:11:08,412
git merge-tree --write-tree 🟢 SAFE: it adds
unreferenced objects and nothing else.

154
00:11:08,752 --> 00:11:13,317
One line of output, a tree ID, and
exit status 0. A tree is not a commit.

155
00:11:13,357 --> 00:11:18,490
Two more plumbing commands, the low-level
kind that do one operation each, turn it

156
00:11:18,530 --> 00:11:20,256
into a merge and publish it.

157
00:11:25,796 --> 00:11:30,318
A merge commit with two parents,
40fb5aa, made without a working tree.

158
00:11:30,358 --> 00:11:33,967
Now a conflicting pair, main
and feature/creative-judge.

159
00:11:34,007 --> 00:11:37,551
Predict the exit status, and
whether a tree is written.

160
00:11:42,391 --> 00:11:47,396
Exit status 1, the three stage entries that
git merge would have put into the index,

161
00:11:47,436 --> 00:11:50,038
and the messages. The
tree is still written.

162
00:11:50,378 --> 00:11:54,720
It contains the file with markers, labelled
with the names from the command line.

163
00:11:55,060 --> 00:12:00,397
--quiet gives a yes or no through the exit
status. --name-only lists the conflicted

164
00:12:00,437 --> 00:12:05,645
files. Decide by the exit status, as the
manual warns, not by whether the list of

165
00:12:05,685 --> 00:12:07,329
conflicted files is empty.

166
00:12:07,669 --> 00:12:11,472
Part 5: a pointer to the third question.
labs/run ch08/revert-merge-preview.

167
00:12:11,512 --> 00:12:15,466
git revert 🟡 refuses a merge commit until
you say which parent is the mainline.

168
00:12:15,506 --> 00:12:19,207
A revert adds a new commit whose change
is the opposite of an earlier one.

169
00:12:27,347 --> 00:12:32,692
-m 1 creates a commit that undoes the
difference between the merge and its first

170
00:12:32,732 --> 00:12:36,125
parent. It undoes content.
It cannot undo ancestry.

171
00:12:36,465 --> 00:12:43,057
That commit is 504ae98. Now Asha adds one
more commit, b752dc4, and the branch is

172
00:12:43,097 --> 00:12:47,315
merged again. Predict what
arrives. Say it out loud.

173
00:12:47,355 --> 00:12:48,134
I'll wait.

174
00:12:52,974 --> 00:12:57,813
Only the new commit arrived. The timeout
is 60, and the two prompt lines of the

175
00:12:57,853 --> 00:12:59,666
first merge did not come back.

176
00:13:00,006 --> 00:13:03,622
The graph says why. Their commits, 5aec6e0
and 58a5e60, are ancestors of main

177
00:13:03,662 --> 00:13:06,898
already, and the merge base is past
them. That is the third question.

178
00:13:06,938 --> 00:13:09,461
The repair is in Chapter 11,
and it has its own video.

179
00:13:11,301 --> 00:13:12,949
Five mistakes to watch for.

180
00:13:13,289 --> 00:13:18,110
Treating "merged without conflicts" as
"tested". Root cause: Git merges snapshots of

181
00:13:18,150 --> 00:13:22,970
text and checks only the overlap of changed
lines; each parent was tested, the merge

182
00:13:23,010 --> 00:13:23,722
tree was not.

183
00:13:24,062 --> 00:13:27,352
Reading git log -p and concluding
that a merge changed nothing.

184
00:13:27,392 --> 00:13:31,051
Root cause: by default git log shows
no diff at all for merge commits.

185
00:13:31,391 --> 00:13:35,715
Trusting an empty git show on a merge. Root
cause: the dense combined format omits

186
00:13:35,755 --> 00:13:39,760
hunks where the result equals one parent,
so a resolution that took one side

187
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:40,984
unchanged is invisible.

188
00:13:41,324 --> 00:13:45,883
Expecting a revert on main to win over
the same change on an unmerged branch.

189
00:13:45,923 --> 00:13:50,662
Root cause: the apply-then-revert pair
cancels out between base and ours, so the

190
00:13:50,702 --> 00:13:53,111
rule table sees "only theirs changed it".

191
00:13:53,451 --> 00:13:57,155
Deciding "can this be merged" from
the list of conflicted files.

192
00:13:57,195 --> 00:14:02,069
Root cause: the manual tells you to decide
by the exit status of git merge-tree, not

193
00:14:02,109 --> 00:14:03,823
by whether that list is empty.

194
00:14:05,663 --> 00:14:09,622
Now, out of the lab. An LLM
evaluation team prepares a release.

195
00:14:09,662 --> 00:14:14,002
Scores on the regression suite moved
slightly and nobody can say why.

196
00:14:14,042 --> 00:14:17,302
Every pull request since
the last release was green.

197
00:14:17,642 --> 00:14:22,374
The release engineer runs one command:
git log --merges --remerge-diff

198
00:14:22,414 --> 00:14:26,465
--first-parent over the range
from the last release to main.

199
00:14:26,505 --> 00:14:31,238
It lists every hand-made change in
every merge since the last release.

200
00:14:31,278 --> 00:14:36,965
Most merges print nothing. One prints a
resolution in the evaluation config, and one

201
00:14:37,005 --> 00:14:41,873
prints a change with no markers nearby.
That is where the review starts.

202
00:14:41,913 --> 00:14:47,532
Lab 6.6 uses the same command to find a
merge where a whole-file --theirs deleted a

203
00:14:47,572 --> 00:14:49,237
teammate's retry setting.

204
00:14:49,577 --> 00:14:54,257
The prevention is in the root-cause box:
test the merge result before it reaches

205
00:14:54,297 --> 00:14:54,552
main.

206
00:14:54,892 --> 00:14:58,933
Across the layer boundary: a server
has no working tree to merge in.

207
00:14:58,973 --> 00:15:03,555
GitHub's engineering blog lists "It can't
check out the repository" among its

208
00:15:03,595 --> 00:15:08,477
requirements for a merge implementation,
and its changelog states that when GitHub

209
00:15:08,517 --> 00:15:13,338
creates merge commits, to test whether a
pull request can be merged cleanly or to

210
00:15:13,378 --> 00:15:16,340
actually merge it, it uses
the merge-ort strategy.

211
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:20,503
Neither page says which command GitHub runs.
git merge-tree is how stock Git exposes

212
00:15:20,543 --> 00:15:23,861
the same strategy without a working
tree. This is described from GitHub's

213
00:15:23,901 --> 00:15:27,586
publications and was not run here. One
documented consequence: workflows will not

214
00:15:27,626 --> 00:15:30,806
run on pull_request activity if the
pull request has a merge conflict.

215
00:15:31,146 --> 00:15:36,257
And the three pointers. rerere, "reuse
recorded resolution", remembers how you

216
00:15:36,297 --> 00:15:41,672
resolved a conflict and replays the resolution
when the identical conflict appears

217
00:15:41,712 --> 00:15:46,427
again. It's off until you set
rerere.enabled, and Chapter 14C covers it.

