00:16:43,362 --> 00:16:48,580
Q122: "Why can Git merge two files
incorrectly from a human perspective?"

232
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:55,769
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
starts from what a merge takes as input and

233
00:16:55,809 --> 00:16:59,764
what single property it checks, and
it says what Git does not have.

234
00:16:59,804 --> 00:17:04,773
It gives at least one concrete example where
the two changes are in different files,

235
00:17:04,813 --> 00:17:09,781
and ideally the quiet kind where nothing
fails. It then moves from cause to control:

236
00:17:09,821 --> 00:17:14,730
where in the delivery process the merged
result gets tested, and how a merge can be

237
00:17:14,770 --> 00:17:18,904
audited afterwards. Stop there. Don't
turn it into a list of commands.

238
00:17:20,744 --> 00:17:23,671
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

239
00:17:24,011 --> 00:17:28,301
A merge without conflicts means only
that no two text changes overlapped.

240
00:17:28,341 --> 00:17:32,986
Two green branches can produce a red merge
when one change depends on the other

241
00:17:33,026 --> 00:17:37,790
through something Git does not read, so
the merge result itself has to be tested.

242
00:17:38,130 --> 00:17:42,775
git log -p shows nothing for a merge, git
show hides resolutions that took one side

243
00:17:42,815 --> 00:17:46,783
unchanged, and --remerge-diff shows
what a human did after Git stopped.

244
00:17:46,823 --> 00:17:51,242
git merge-tree --write-tree computes a
merge from the object database alone and

245
00:17:51,282 --> 00:17:55,814
reports the outcome through its exit status.
Reverting a merge undoes content and

246
00:17:55,854 --> 00:18:00,274
not ancestry, which is why a later merge
of the same branch brings only the new

247
00:18:00,314 --> 00:18:00,725
commits.

